# docs/KEYMAP.md §Dialogs and text inputs — "A multi-line input publishes
# `enter = newline | owner` … The agent composer publishes `owner`, so its plain `Enter` reaches
# Send/Steer", while `Shift-Enter` inserts in every multi-line input.
# docs/KEYMAP.md §Native agent thread — `^s a` starts a Claude thread whose composer is focused.
#
# The composer's buffer is not projected — the snapshot carries threads, not drafts — so the
# edits are read back through what was actually sent: the tab label is the submitted prompt
# (`scenarios/agents/thread-streaming.scenario` pins that), which is the only harness-visible
# oracle for composer text. For the same reason the `up` history recall is not asserted here:
# a recalled draft is a buffer, and nothing in version 1 of the snapshot shows it.
#
# Paste is deliberately not exercised: `clipboard set` and `clipboard get` fail in every lane by
# design (`docs/TESTING-HARNESS.md` §1); the kit's own gpui tests cover `cmd-v`.
#
# No `shot`: this is a keyboard scenario, so it belongs in the headless subset too (§7).
fixture: agents
await idle
key o
await screen == Workspace 20000
key ctrl-s a
await agents.threads[0].provider == claude 20000
assert mode == Agent && focused == agents.composer
await idle 30000

# The composer is reached with the keyboard rather than the pointer: `targets["agents.composer"]`
# covers the whole composer column — the editor row, the host badge and the metadata strip — so
# the centre a named `click` lands on is below the editor, and typing after such a click reaches
# nothing while `focused` still answers `agents.composer`. That is a defect of the surface, not
# of this scenario, so it is reported rather than pinned here.
assert targets["agents.composer"] exists && key_contexts[1] == AgentIdle

# Two words, then `alt-backspace` takes the second one back.
type explain this crate
key alt-backspace
type module

# `Shift-Enter` inserts a newline instead of sending: the thread is still idle afterwards, and
# the composer still owns the keyboard.
key shift-enter
type please
assert agents.threads[0].state == idle && focused == agents.composer
assert lists.tabs.selected.label == Claude

# Plain `Enter` is the owner's: it sends the two-line draft, and the tab takes its first line.
key enter
await agents.threads[0].state == working 20000
assert lists.tabs.selected.label ~= "^explain this module"
assert key_contexts[1] == AgentWorking
dump sent

# The turn settles normally, so nothing about the edited draft left the thread stuck.
await agents.threads[0].state == idle 40000
assert key_contexts[1] == AgentIdle && sticky_error absent
assert lists.tabs.selected.label ~= "^explain this module"
dump finished
quit
